/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Ecclesiastes chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("ecclesiastes", [
{n:1, name:"Vanity of Vanities",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:2",verse:"Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity."},
 facts:["Did you know the word 'vanity' renders the Hebrew hebel, meaning breath or vapor, the same word used for the fleeting life of Abel in Genesis 4 (Ecclesiastes 1:2)?","Did you know the phrase 'under the sun' appears about thirty times in Ecclesiastes, framing everything as life examined without eternity in view (Ecclesiastes 1:3)?","Did you know 'there is no new thing under the sun' was written nearly three thousand years ago, making it one of the oldest recorded complaints about the news cycle (Ecclesiastes 1:9)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"How does Scripture introduce 'the Preacher'?", options:["The son of David, king in Jerusalem","A prophet from the tribe of Judah","A priest serving in the temple","A merchant grown rich in Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:1",verse:"The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem.", insight:"The writer is no armchair philosopher but a king with a crown and a record; when he calls life vanity, he has tested every alternative first."},
{q:"What phrase sums up the Preacher's verdict on everything?", options:["Vanity of vanities; all is vanity","Blessed are the pure in heart","The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom","To every thing there is a season"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:2",verse:"Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher, vanity of vanities; all is vanity.", insight:"Vanity here is breath, vapor, here and gone; the Preacher names the feeling honestly instead of dressing it up, and honesty is where wisdom begins."},
{q:"What question does he ask about a man's labour?", options:["What profit hath a man of all his labour which he taketh under the sun?","Who will remember a man's labour after him?","How long shall a man's labour endure?","Where shall a man's labour be rewarded?"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:3",verse:"What profit hath a man of all his labour which he taketh under the sun?", insight:"Notice the boundary: under the sun. The question is not whether work matters, but whether work can carry the weight of meaning all by itself."},
{q:"What abides for ever while generations pass away and come?", options:["The earth","The king's palace","The temple in Jerusalem","The wealth of the rich"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:4",verse:"One generation passeth away, and another generation cometh: but the earth abideth for ever.", insight:"Generations come and go like guests, but the house remains; the Preacher is struck by how little of us stays behind."},
{q:"What does the sun do each day?", options:["Ariseth, goeth down, and hasteth to his place where he arose","Rests at noon and rises at midnight","Stands still over Jerusalem","Fades and is renewed each month"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:5",verse:"The sun also ariseth, and the sun goeth down, and hasteth to his place where he arose.", insight:"The sun works the hardest commute in creation and never arrives anywhere new; effort without destination is the Preacher's first picture of vanity."},
{q:"Which way does the wind travel in its circuits?", options:["Toward the south, then about unto the north, whirling continually","From the east straight to the west","From the mountains down to the sea","In a single line that never returns"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:6",verse:"The wind goeth toward the south, and turneth about unto the north; it whirleth about continually, and the wind returneth again according to his circuits.", insight:"The wind's route is a circle with no finish line; some of our busiest seasons feel exactly like this."},
{q:"What happens to all the rivers?", options:["They run into the sea, yet the sea is not full, and they return to their source","They dry up before reaching the sea","They fill the sea until it overflows","They turn back at the shoreline"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:7",verse:"All the rivers run into the sea; yet the sea is not full; unto the place from whence the rivers come, thither they return again.", insight:"Rivers pour themselves out and the sea never fills; giving everything to something that cannot hold it is a quiet kind of grief."},
{q:"Which two things are never satisfied?", options:["The eye with seeing, and the ear with hearing","The hand with working, and the foot with walking","The mouth with speaking, and the heart with loving","The mind with thinking, and the soul with praying"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:8",verse:"All things are full of labour; man cannot utter it: the eye is not satisfied with seeing, nor the ear filled with hearing.", insight:"Appetites do not retire when fed; they come back hungry, which is why satisfaction can never be the finish line."},
{q:"What does the Preacher claim about new things?", options:["There is no new thing under the sun","Every generation invents its own wisdom","New things come with each sunrise","The future holds what the past never knew"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:9",verse:"The thing that hath been, it is that which shall be; and that which is done is that which shall be done: and there is no new thing under the sun.", insight:"Every generation thinks it invented both trouble and brilliance; the Preacher gently suggests we are reruns with better lighting."},
{q:"When, according to the Preacher, had so-called new things already existed?", options:["Of old time, which was before us","In the days of the judges only","After the building of the temple","In the latter days alone"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:10",verse:"Is there any thing whereof it may be said, See, this is new? it hath been already of old time, which was before us.", insight:"What looks brand new is usually just well forgotten; humility remembers that we are late arrivals to an old story."},
{q:"Of what is there no remembrance?", options:["Of former things, nor of things to come with those that come after","Of the kings and their decrees","Of the harvests and the rains","Of the songs of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:11",verse:"There is no remembrance of former things; neither shall there be any remembrance of things that are to come with those that shall come after.", insight:"Being forgotten is the great equalizer; the Preacher finds it sobering, not bitter, because it frees us from performing for posterity."},
{q:"Over whom does the Preacher say he was king?", options:["Over Israel in Jerusalem","Over Judah in Hebron","Over Egypt in Zoan","Over Edom in Petra"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:12",verse:"I the Preacher was king over Israel in Jerusalem.", insight:"He is not guessing about power; he held the throne in the holy city and still found it light as breath."},
{q:"To what did the Preacher give his heart?", options:["To seek and search out by wisdom concerning all things done under heaven","To gather silver and gold from the provinces","To build cities and plant vineyards","To number the stars and name them"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:13",verse:"And I gave my heart to seek and search out by wisdom concerning all things that are done under heaven: this sore travail hath God given to the sons of man to be exercised therewith.", insight:"Even the search itself is called sore travail, hard labor assigned by God; the quest for meaning is work, not entertainment."},
{q:"What did he call all the works done under the sun?", options:["Vanity and vexation of spirit","A crown of glory and honor","The portion of the righteous","A light unto the nations"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:14",verse:"I have seen all the works that are done under the sun; and, behold, all is vanity and vexation of spirit.", insight:"Vexation of spirit is chasing the wind with your arms open; the Preacher tried to catch it so we would not have to."},
{q:"What cannot be made straight?", options:["That which is crooked","That which is broken in battle","That which is lost at sea","That which is hidden in darkness"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:15",verse:"That which is crooked cannot be made straight: and that which is wanting cannot be numbered.", insight:"Some things in life will not unbend no matter how we push; wisdom learns to name the crooked rather than deny it."},
{q:"What did the Preacher claim about his own wisdom?", options:["He had gotten more wisdom than all before him in Jerusalem","He had less wisdom than his father David","He had hidden his wisdom from the people","He had traded his wisdom for riches"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:16",verse:"I communed with mine own heart, saying, Lo, I am come to great estate, and have gotten more wisdom than all they that have been before me in Jerusalem: yea, my heart had great experience of wisdom and knowledge.", insight:"His credentials were unmatched and his conclusion was grief; more knowing did not make him more settled."},
{q:"Besides wisdom, what did he give his heart to know?", options:["Madness and folly","War and conquest","Trade and navigation","Poetry and music"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:17",verse:"And I gave my heart to know wisdom, and to know madness and folly: I perceived that this also is vexation of spirit.", insight:"He studied both the library and the gutter to be thorough; thoroughness still ended in vexation."},
{q:"What comes with much wisdom?", options:["Much grief","Much honor","Much wealth","Much rest"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:18",verse:"For in much wisdom is much grief: and he that increaseth knowledge increaseth sorrow.", insight:"The more clearly you see what is broken, the more it hurts; knowledge is a gift that comes with heavier eyes."},
{q:"What increases along with knowledge?", options:["Sorrow","Strength","Favor","Peace"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:18",verse:"For in much wisdom is much grief: and he that increaseth knowledge increaseth sorrow.", insight:"Every new fact is also a new weight; the Preacher is honest that learning has a cost, not just a shine."},
{q:"In what order does the wind travel its circuits?", options:["South, then north, whirling about continually and returning again","North, then south, then resting in the east","East, then west, then rising upward","Upward, then downward, then standing still"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 1:6",verse:"The wind goeth toward the south, and turneth about unto the north; it whirleth about continually, and the wind returneth again according to his circuits.", insight:"South, north, then round and round in its circuits; the order never changes, which is exactly the Preacher's point."}
]},
{n:2, name:"The Pleasure Experiment",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:24",verse:"There is nothing better for a man, than that he should eat and drink, and that he should make his soul enjoy good in his labour. This also I saw, that it was from the hand of God."},
 facts:["Did you know the Preacher tested pleasure the way a scientist runs an experiment, deliberately and thoroughly, before writing down his verdict (Ecclesiastes 2:1)?","Did you know his list of projects includes houses, vineyards, gardens, orchards, pools of water, cattle, silver, gold, and singers with instruments of every sort (Ecclesiastes 2:8)?","Did you know the first bright note in the book lands at verse 24, where enjoying your work is called a gift 'from the hand of God' (Ecclesiastes 2:24)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"How did the Preacher first 'prove' his heart?", options:["With mirth, bidding it enjoy pleasure","With fasting in the wilderness","With silence in the temple","With study of the law"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:1",verse:"I said in mine heart, Go to now, I will prove thee with mirth, therefore enjoy pleasure: and, behold, this also is vanity.", insight:"He ran a deliberate experiment on pleasure, not a careless stumble; even his fun was research, and research needs honest results."},
{q:"What did he say of laughter?", options:["It is mad","It is holy","It is wise","It is needful"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:2",verse:"I said of laughter, It is mad: and of mirth, What doeth it?", insight:"Laughter promised joy and delivered noise; calling it mad is the verdict of a man who laughed his way through the data."},
{q:"What question did he ask of mirth?", options:["What doeth it?","Whence cometh it?","Who gave it?","How long shall it last?"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:2",verse:"I said of laughter, It is mad: and of mirth, What doeth it?", insight:"Mirth works hard at the party and accomplishes nothing by morning; the question 'what doeth it' still answers itself."},
{q:"What did he give himself to while still 'acquainting' his heart with wisdom?", options:["Wine","Rich foods","Fine clothing","Long journeys"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:3",verse:"I sought in mine heart to give myself unto wine, yet acquainting mine heart with wisdom; and to lay hold on folly, till I might see what was that good for the sons of men, which they should do under the heaven all the days of their life.", insight:"He tried to stay thoughtful while drinking deep, steering the experiment with one sober hand; divided hearts rarely find whole answers."},
{q:"What great works did he make first?", options:["Houses, and vineyards planted","Walls, and towers raised","Ships, and harbors built","Roads, and bridges laid"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:4",verse:"I made me great works; I builded me houses; I planted me vineyards:", insight:"He built on the grandest scale a king can; if building could fill a soul, his soul would have been full."},
{q:"What did he plant 'of all kind of fruits'?", options:["Trees, in gardens and orchards he made","Vines along the city walls","Herbs in the temple courts","Reeds beside the river"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:5",verse:"I made me gardens and orchards, and I planted trees in them of all kind of fruits:", insight:"He collected pleasures the way others collect stamps, every variety; abundance of options is not the same as abundance of joy."},
{q:"Why did he make pools of water?", options:["To water the wood that bringeth forth trees","To supply the city in drought","To bathe the royal horses","To flood the neighboring fields"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:6",verse:"I made me pools of water, to water therewith the wood that bringeth forth trees:", insight:"He engineered his own paradise with reservoirs; when you must irrigate your happiness, the happiness may not be real."},
{q:"In what did he surpass all before him in Jerusalem?", options:["Great possessions of great and small cattle","Chariots and horsemen of war","Grain stored in royal barns","Ships trading with distant lands"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:7",verse:"I got me servants and maidens, and had servants born in my house; also I had great possessions of great and small cattle above all that were in Jerusalem before me:", insight:"He out-owned every king before him in Jerusalem and kept score; comparison is a treadmill that speeds up the more you win."},
{q:"What precious things did he gather?", options:["Silver and gold, and the peculiar treasure of kings and of the provinces","Pearls and ivory from the coast","Spices and perfumes from the east","Bronze and iron for the armory"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:8",verse:"I gathered me also silver and gold, and the peculiar treasure of kings and of the provinces: I gat me men singers and women singers, and the delights of the sons of men, as musical instruments, and that of all sorts.", insight:"He gathered the treasuries of kings and provinces; wealth kept its shine everywhere except the one place he needed it, his heart."},
{q:"Who provided his music?", options:["Men singers and women singers","The priests of the temple","His own sons and daughters","Hired soldiers of the guard"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:8",verse:"I gathered me also silver and gold, and the peculiar treasure of kings and of the provinces: I gat me men singers and women singers, and the delights of the sons of men, as musical instruments, and that of all sorts.", insight:"He hired the soundtrack of joy, men's and women's voices with instruments of every sort; music can lift a mood but it cannot anchor a life."},
{q:"What remained with him through it all?", options:["His wisdom","His youth","His health","His friends"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:9",verse:"So I was great, and increased more than all that were before me in Jerusalem: also my wisdom remained with me.", insight:"Through every excess his wisdom stayed with him, which is why his verdict counts; a fool's warning about folly means little, a wise man's means much."},
{q:"What did he keep back from his eyes?", options:["Nothing; whatsoever they desired he kept not from them","Only what the law forbade","Wealth, lest it corrupt him","Pleasure, lest it distract him"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:10",verse:"And whatsoever mine eyes desired I kept not from them, I withheld not my heart from any joy; for my heart rejoiced in all my labour: and this was my portion of all my labour.", insight:"He denied himself nothing and found the everything empty; total indulgence is the fastest way to prove pleasure's limits."},
{q:"After reviewing all his works, what was his verdict?", options:["All was vanity and vexation of spirit, with no profit under the sun","All was good and worthy of remembrance","All was profitable to the kingdom","All was pleasing in God's sight"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:11",verse:"Then I looked on all the works that my hands had wrought, and on the labour that I had laboured to do: and, behold, all was vanity and vexation of spirit, and there was no profit under the sun.", insight:"He looked at the finished portfolio and wrote 'no profit'; when the richest man calls riches empty, the numbers deserve respect."},
{q:"How far does wisdom excel folly?", options:["As far as light excelleth darkness","As far as gold excelleth silver","As far as the heaven is above the earth","As far as summer excelleth winter"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:13",verse:"Then I saw that wisdom excelleth folly, as far as light excelleth darkness.", insight:"Wisdom is genuinely better than folly, the way light beats darkness; the Preacher is not saying nothing matters, only that everything fades."},
{q:"Where are the wise man's eyes?", options:["In his head","On the horizon","In the law of God","Toward the heavens"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:14",verse:"The wise man’s eyes are in his head; but the fool walketh in darkness: and I myself perceived also that one event happeneth to them all.", insight:"The wise man sees where he walks; seeing clearly is still an advantage, even when the road ends at the same grave."},
{q:"How does the wise man die?", options:["As the fool","In honor above all men","With his wisdom intact","Remembered for ever"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:16",verse:"For there is no remembrance of the wise more than of the fool for ever; seeing that which now is in the days to come shall all be forgotten. And how dieth the wise man? as the fool.", insight:"Death does not grade on wisdom; the same forgetfulness covers the brilliant and the foolish, which stung him deeply."},
{q:"Why did he hate all his labour under the sun?", options:["Because he must leave it to the man that shall come after him","Because it earned him no wages","Because the people despised it","Because it broke his health"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:18",verse:"Yea, I hated all my labour which I had taken under the sun: because I should leave it unto the man that shall be after me.", insight:"The thought of a stranger spending his life's work soured the work itself; legacy is a shaky foundation for meaning."},
{q:"What troubled him about the heir to his labour?", options:["Who knows whether he shall be a wise man or a fool","Whether he would live in Jerusalem","Whether he would keep the singers","Whether he would pay the servants"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:19",verse:"And who knoweth whether he shall be a wise man or a fool? yet shall he have rule over all my labour wherein I have laboured, and wherein I have shewed myself wise under the sun. This is also vanity.", insight:"He could not control who inherited his empire of effort; the unknowable heir is the argument against living for legacy."},
{q:"What are 'all his days' for the toiling man?", options:["Sorrows, and his travail grief, with no rest even in the night","Joy, and his rest complete","Profit, and his barns full","Honor, and his name praised"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:23",verse:"For all his days are sorrows, and his travail grief; yea, his heart taketh not rest in the night. This is also vanity.", insight:"Work followed him into the night and stole his rest; when labor owns your sleep, it owns too much."},
{q:"What is 'nothing better' for a man than, and whose hand is it from?", options:["To eat and drink and enjoy good in his labour, from the hand of God","To gather riches and store them, from his own hand","To seek wisdom above all, from the hand of teachers","To rest from all labour, from the hand of time"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 2:24",verse:"There is nothing better for a man, than that he should eat and drink, and that he should make his soul enjoy good in his labour. This also I saw, that it was from the hand of God.", insight:"Here the book turns toward light: enjoying your work is not settling for less, it is receiving a gift from God's own hand."}
]},
{n:3, name:"A Time for Everything",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:11",verse:"He hath made every thing beautiful in his time: also he hath set the world in their heart, so that no man can find out the work that God maketh from the beginning to the end."},
 facts:["Did you know the famous 'time for every purpose' poem packs fourteen pairs of opposites into just seven verses, tracing a whole human life from birth to death (Ecclesiastes 3:2)?","Did you know 'he hath set the world in their heart' uses a Hebrew word for eternity, meaning every person carries a sense of forever that this world cannot fill (Ecclesiastes 3:11)?","Did you know the line 'a time to keep silence, and a time to speak' has echoed through sermons and speeches for centuries (Ecclesiastes 3:7)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What belongs to every thing?", options:["A season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven","A price, and a buyer under the sun","A beginning, and an end in the earth","A master, and a servant in the house"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:1",verse:"To every thing there is a season, and a time to every purpose under the heaven:", insight:"Before listing a single pair, he states the principle: life has appointments; wisdom is showing up for the right one at the right hour."},
{q:"How many 'times' are named in the verse about being born and dying?", options:["Four","Two","Six","Eight"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:2",verse:"A time to be born, and a time to die; a time to plant, and a time to pluck up that which is planted;", insight:"Birth and death bookend planting and uprooting, as if to say our whole span, from first breath to last harvest, is timed by Another."},
{q:"Which pairs involve killing and breaking down?", options:["A time to kill and a time to heal; a time to break down and a time to build up","A time to weep and a time to laugh; a time to mourn and a time to dance","A time to get and a time to lose; a time to keep and a time to cast away","A time to love and a time to hate; a time of war and a time of peace"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:3",verse:"A time to kill, and a time to heal; a time to break down, and a time to build up;", insight:"Healing and building sit beside killing and breaking in the same verse; the same hands that tear down are given hours to repair."},
{q:"Which pairs involve weeping and mourning?", options:["A time to weep and a time to laugh; a time to mourn and a time to dance","A time to rend and a time to sew; a time to keep silence and a time to speak","A time to cast away stones and a time to gather stones together","A time to embrace and a time to refrain from embracing"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:4",verse:"A time to weep, and a time to laugh; a time to mourn, and a time to dance;", insight:"Tears and laughter are both scheduled; grief is not the opposite of joy here, it is its appointed neighbor."},
{q:"What does the verse say about stones and embracing?", options:["A time to cast away stones and gather them; a time to embrace and refrain from embracing","A time to lift stones and lay them; a time to greet and depart","A time to count stones and weigh them; a time to hold fast and release","A time to break stones and shape them; a time to arrive and farewell"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:5",verse:"A time to cast away stones, and a time to gather stones together; a time to embrace, and a time to refrain from embracing;", insight:"Even stones have their gathering and scattering, and arms their holding and releasing; nothing is always, everything is sometimes."},
{q:"In what order do getting, losing, keeping, and casting away appear?", options:["Get, lose, keep, cast away","Lose, get, cast away, keep","Keep, get, lose, cast away","Get, keep, lose, cast away"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:6",verse:"A time to get, and a time to lose; a time to keep, and a time to cast away;", insight:"Getting and losing, keeping and throwing away, in that steady rhythm; holding loosely is the skill this verse is quietly teaching."},
{q:"Which pairs involve rending and silence?", options:["A time to rend and a time to sew; a time to keep silence and a time to speak","A time to tear down and a time to build; a time to cry and a time to sing","A time to break and a time to mend; a time to hide and a time to seek","A time to cut and a time to join; a time to whisper and a time to shout"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:7",verse:"A time to rend, and a time to sew; a time to keep silence, and a time to speak;", insight:"There is a time to tear and a time to mend, a time to hush and a time to speak up; wisdom knows which hour it is."},
{q:"Which two pairs close the poem?", options:["A time to love and a time to hate; a time of war and a time of peace","A time to live and a time to die; a time to sow and a time to reap","A time to give and a time to receive; a time to work and a time to rest","A time to hope and a time to despair; a time to trust and a time to doubt"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:8",verse:"A time to love, and a time to hate; a time of war, and a time of peace.", insight:"The poem ends at the largest scale, nations loving and hating, warring and resting; even history runs on heaven's timetable."},
{q:"What question follows the poem?", options:["What profit hath he that worketh in that wherein he laboureth?","Who shall declare the times unto men?","How long shall the seasons endure?","Where shall the labourer find rest?"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:9",verse:"What profit hath he that worketh in that wherein he laboureth?", insight:"After the beautiful poem comes the blunt question again; beauty alone does not answer it, which is why the question keeps returning."},
{q:"What has God given the sons of men to be exercised in?", options:["The travail He has given them","The wisdom of the ages","The riches of the earth","The silence of the night"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:10",verse:"I have seen the travail, which God hath given to the sons of men to be exercised in it.", insight:"God gives the travail as exercise, not punishment; the weight is meant to strengthen the soul that carries it."},
{q:"When is every thing beautiful?", options:["In his time","In the spring","In the morning","In the latter days"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:11",verse:"He hath made every thing beautiful in his time: also he hath set the world in their heart, so that no man can find out the work that God maketh from the beginning to the end.", insight:"Beautiful, but in His time, not ours; much of our frustration is just quarreling with the schedule."},
{q:"What has God set in man's heart?", options:["The world","A compass","A lamp","A seed"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:11",verse:"He hath made every thing beautiful in his time: also he hath set the world in their heart, so that no man can find out the work that God maketh from the beginning to the end.", insight:"God planted eternity in the human heart, so no earthly portion ever fits; that ache for forever was put there on purpose."},
{q:"What good does the Preacher find in a man's life?", options:["To rejoice, and to do good in his life","To gather, and to store up riches","To rule, and to be served","To travel, and to see wonders"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:12",verse:"I know that there is no good in them, but for a man to rejoice, and to do good in his life.", insight:"In the middle of the mystery, simple counsel: rejoice and do good; the small obedient life is never wasted."},
{q:"What is the gift of God?", options:["That every man should eat and drink, and enjoy the good of all his labour","That kings should reign in righteousness","That the poor should be lifted up","That the wise should be remembered"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:13",verse:"And also that every man should eat and drink, and enjoy the good of all his labour, it is the gift of God.", insight:"Enjoying your dinner and your day's work is called a gift, not a guilt; gratitude is the doorway the Preacher keeps pointing to."},
{q:"How long shall whatsoever God does endure?", options:["For ever","For a generation","Until the jubilee","While the sun shines"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:14",verse:"I know that, whatsoever God doeth, it shall be for ever: nothing can be put to it, nor any thing taken from it: and God doeth it, that men should fear before him.", insight:"What God does cannot be improved by adding or reduced by taking; permanence belongs to Him alone, which steadies the anxious heart."},
{q:"Why does God do what He does?", options:["That men should fear before him","That men should question him","That men should forget him","That men should replace him"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:14",verse:"I know that, whatsoever God doeth, it shall be for ever: nothing can be put to it, nor any thing taken from it: and God doeth it, that men should fear before him.", insight:"God's unchangeable work is meant to produce reverence; awe is the right response to a God who cannot be edited."},
{q:"Who requires that which is past?", options:["God","The king","The priests","The elders"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:15",verse:"That which hath been is now; and that which is to be hath already been; and God requireth that which is past.", insight:"Nothing slips off God's books, even the past; the Judge keeps perfect records, which is both warning and comfort."},
{q:"What did the Preacher see in the place of judgment?", options:["Wickedness was there; and in the place of righteousness, iniquity","Justice done swiftly; and mercy shown freely","The righteous rewarded; and the wicked punished","Peace established; and truth restored"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:16",verse:"And moreover I saw under the sun the place of judgment, that wickedness was there; and the place of righteousness, that iniquity was there.", insight:"Corruption in the courthouse is the oldest news; the Preacher saw it too, and refused to let it have the last word."},
{q:"What will God do with the righteous and the wicked?", options:["Judge them, for there is a time for every purpose and every work","Reward both alike","Forget them both","Leave them to their fate"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:17",verse:"I said in mine heart, God shall judge the righteous and the wicked: for there is a time there for every purpose and for every work.", insight:"The verdict on crooked judges is certain, and it comes with its own appointed time; justice delayed is not justice denied."},
{q:"What is a man's portion?", options:["To rejoice in his own works","To know what comes after him","To leave riches to his sons","To be praised by his neighbors"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 3:22",verse:"Wherefore I perceive that there is nothing better, than that a man should rejoice in his own works; for that is his portion: for who shall bring him to see what shall be after him?", insight:"Your portion is the work in front of you, done with joy; the future is God's business, the present is yours."}
]},
{n:4, name:"Two Are Better Than One",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:9",verse:"Two are better than one; because they have a good reward for their labour."},
 facts:["Did you know a three-strand cord really does hold far more than three single strands, which is why weavers and sailors still trust the Preacher's physics (Ecclesiastes 4:12)?","Did you know the fool 'folding his hands' is the classic posture of laziness in Proverbs, a man hugging himself while his life slips away (Ecclesiastes 4:5)?","Did you know the chapter opens with the loneliest sight on earth, oppressed people with no comforter, and answers it with the warmest: two are better than one (Ecclesiastes 4:9)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What did the Preacher return to consider?", options:["All the oppressions done under the sun","All the riches gathered under heaven","All the songs sung in the temple","All the wars fought by the kings"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:1",verse:"So I returned, and considered all the oppressions that are done under the sun: and behold the tears of such as were oppressed, and they had no comforter; and on the side of their oppressors there was power; but they had no comforter.", insight:"He looked at oppression with open eyes and named the tears; Scripture never asks us to look away from suffering to keep our faith tidy."},
{q:"What did the oppressed lack, and what stood with their oppressors?", options:["No comforter for the oppressed; power on the side of the oppressors","No bread for the oppressed; swords with the oppressors","No shelter for the oppressed; horses with the oppressors","No law for the oppressed; judges with the oppressors"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:1",verse:"So I returned, and considered all the oppressions that are done under the sun: and behold the tears of such as were oppressed, and they had no comforter; and on the side of their oppressors there was power; but they had no comforter.", insight:"The saddest arithmetic in the chapter: tears on one side, power on the other, and no comforter anywhere; the Preacher records the imbalance without flinching."},
{q:"Whom did he praise more than the living?", options:["The dead which are already dead","The kings which reign in glory","The rich which prosper greatly","The wise which teach the people"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:2",verse:"Wherefore I praised the dead which are already dead more than the living which are yet alive.", insight:"Grief can make death look kinder than life; the Preacher voices the thought honestly rather than letting it fester in silence."},
{q:"Who is better off than both the dead and the living?", options:["He which hath not yet been, who hath not seen the evil work done under the sun","He which is young and strong","He which is rich and honored","He which dwells in a far country"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:3",verse:"Yea, better is he than both they, which hath not yet been, who hath not seen the evil work that is done under the sun.", insight:"He says the unborn are better off than both, a dark sentence; it is the cry of a heart overwhelmed, not a doctrine to build on."},
{q:"For what is a man envied of his neighbour?", options:["For all travail, and every right work","For his lands and houses","For his sons and daughters","For his favor with the king"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:4",verse:"Again, I considered all travail, and every right work, that for this a man is envied of his neighbour. This is also vanity and vexation of spirit.", insight:"Even good work breeds envy in the watcher; when your success becomes your neighbor's sore spot, the vanity is in the rivalry, not the work."},
{q:"What does the fool do with his hands?", options:["Foldeth them together, and eateth his own flesh","Lifteth them in prayer","Washeth them in innocence","Hideth them in his bosom"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:5",verse:"The fool foldeth his hands together, and eateth his own flesh.", insight:"Idleness is not rest; folded hands consume the man they belong to, which is why laziness is never the harmless option."},
{q:"What is better than both hands full with travail?", options:["An handful with quietness","A house full of riches","A table full of feasting","A field full of harvest"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:6",verse:"Better is an handful with quietness, than both the hands full with travail and vexation of spirit.", insight:"One handful held in peace outweighs two fists full of striving; enough with quiet is the wealth the striver never finds."},
{q:"What did the Preacher see when he returned and looked again?", options:["Vanity under the sun","Glory in the heavens","Wisdom among the elders","Justice in the gates"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:7",verse:"Then I returned, and I saw vanity under the sun.", insight:"He keeps returning and looking again; honest examination, repeated, is how the Preacher does his theology."},
{q:"What question does the lonely rich man never ask?", options:["For whom do I labour, and bereave my soul of good?","When shall my riches be complete?","Who shall guard my treasures?","How shall I spend my wealth?"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:8",verse:"There is one alone, and there is not a second; yea, he hath neither child nor brother: yet is there no end of all his labour; neither is his eye satisfied with riches; neither saith he, For whom do I labour, and bereave my soul of good? This is also vanity, yea, it is a sore travail.", insight:"The loneliest question in the book: for whom am I doing all this; work without a beloved at the end of it turns hollow."},
{q:"What is never satisfied in the solitary man?", options:["His eye with riches","His ear with music","His mouth with food","His heart with love"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:8",verse:"There is one alone, and there is not a second; yea, he hath neither child nor brother: yet is there no end of all his labour; neither is his eye satisfied with riches; neither saith he, For whom do I labour, and bereave my soul of good? This is also vanity, yea, it is a sore travail.", insight:"His eye never said enough; riches have a way of moving the finish line every time you approach it."},
{q:"Why are two better than one?", options:["Because they have a good reward for their labour","Because they are stronger than three","Because they never disagree","Because they share one purse"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:9",verse:"Two are better than one; because they have a good reward for their labour.", insight:"Two are better than one, not sentimentally but economically: shared labor pays better dividends, and shared life does too."},
{q:"What happens if the two fall?", options:["The one will lift up his fellow","Both will lie in the dust","The stronger will blame the weaker","They will call for a stranger"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:10",verse:"For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falleth; for he hath not another to help him up.", insight:"A friend is someone who reaches down when you fall; the verse treats this as ordinary equipment for the journey, not a luxury."},
{q:"What is the woe of him that is alone?", options:["When he falleth, he hath not another to help him up","When he sleepeth, he hath no dream","When he eateth, he hath no bread","When he speaketh, he hath no answer"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:10",verse:"For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow: but woe to him that is alone when he falleth; for he hath not another to help him up.", insight:"Aloneness is not strength, it is exposure; the woe is not a curse but a warning about traveling solo."},
{q:"How can one be warm alone?", options:["He cannot; but if two lie together, they have heat","By heaping coals upon himself","By wrapping in many blankets","By staying near the fire"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:11",verse:"Again, if two lie together, then they have heat: but how can one be warm alone?", insight:"Warmth is a shared commodity in a cold world; the question answers itself, and the answer is an invitation."},
{q:"What is not quickly broken?", options:["A threefold cord","A single thread","A doubled rope","An iron chain"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:12",verse:"And if one prevail against him, two shall withstand him; and a threefold cord is not quickly broken.", insight:"Three strands hold far beyond the sum of one plus one plus one; some strength only exists in togetherness."},
{q:"What happens if one prevail against a man?", options:["Two shall withstand him","He shall flee to the hills","He shall pay a ransom","He shall hide in a cave"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:12",verse:"And if one prevail against him, two shall withstand him; and a threefold cord is not quickly broken.", insight:"One against an attacker is a victim, two is a defense; God designed us to be harder to break in company."},
{q:"Who is better than an old and foolish king?", options:["A poor and a wise child","A rich and mighty prince","A strong and swift captain","A learned and aged scribe"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:13",verse:"Better is a poor and a wise child than an old and foolish king, who will no more be admonished.", insight:"Wisdom outranks the crown when the crown stops listening; teachability is worth more than a throne."},
{q:"What makes the old king foolish?", options:["He will no more be admonished","He has grown weak with age","He has lost his treasures","He has forgotten the law"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:13",verse:"Better is a poor and a wise child than an old and foolish king, who will no more be admonished.", insight:"The king's folly is not age but closed ears; the moment you cannot be corrected, you have started to fall."},
{q:"From where does the poor wise child come to reign?", options:["Out of prison","Out of the wilderness","Out of a far country","Out of the temple courts"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:14",verse:"For out of prison he cometh to reign; whereas also he that is born in his kingdom becometh poor.", insight:"From prison to palace in one promotion; God has always specialized in unlikely elevations."},
{q:"What will those who come after do regarding the popular successor?", options:["They shall not rejoice in him","They shall crown him again","They shall write songs of him","They shall build him a monument"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 4:16",verse:"There is no end of all the people, even of all that have been before them: they also that come after shall not rejoice in him. Surely this also is vanity and vexation of spirit.", insight:"Crowds cheer the rising star and forget him by the next parade; popularity is the thinnest currency in the chapter."}
]},
{n:5, name:"Keep Thy Foot",
 seal:{ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:2",verse:"Be not rash with thy mouth, and let not thine heart be hasty to utter any thing before God: for God is in heaven, and thou upon earth: therefore let thy words be few."},
 facts:["Did you know 'keep thy foot when thou goest to the house of God' is Scripture's way of saying slow down and listen before you speak or promise in worship (Ecclesiastes 5:1)?","Did you know the warning about vows reaches back to Deuteronomy and forward to Jesus' teaching on oaths in the Sermon on the Mount (Ecclesiastes 5:5)?","Did you know the working man's sweet sleep versus the rich man's restless nights is one of the most quoted pictures of contentment in all of Scripture (Ecclesiastes 5:12)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What should you do when you go to the house of God?", options:["Keep thy foot, and be more ready to hear than to give the sacrifice of fools","Bring many gifts and offer them quickly","Speak many words and pray loudly","Come rarely and stay briefly"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:1",verse:"Keep thy foot when thou goest to the house of God, and be more ready to hear, than to give the sacrifice of fools: for they consider not that they do evil.", insight:"Slow feet and open ears at the house of God; worship begins with listening, not with performing."},
{q:"Why is the sacrifice of fools evil?", options:["They consider not that they do evil","They bring too little","They come at the wrong hour","They sing out of tune"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:1",verse:"Keep thy foot when thou goest to the house of God, and be more ready to hear, than to give the sacrifice of fools: for they consider not that they do evil.", insight:"Fools bring offerings without noticing they are doing evil; sincerity without self-examination is still sincerity, but it is not worship."},
{q:"Why should your words be few before God?", options:["For God is in heaven, and thou upon earth","For the priests are listening","For the congregation is waiting","For the hour is late"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:2",verse:"Be not rash with thy mouth, and let not thine heart be hasty to utter any thing before God: for God is in heaven, and thou upon earth: therefore let thy words be few.", insight:"God is in heaven and you are on earth; the distance is not coldness, it is a reminder to speak like you mean it."},
{q:"How is a fool's voice known?", options:["By multitude of words","By loudness of crying","By swiftness of speech","By softness of whispering"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:3",verse:"For a dream cometh through the multitude of business; and a fool’s voice is known by multitude of words.", insight:"Word count is the fool's tell; the wise measure their speech because they know words have weight."},
{q:"What should you do when you vow a vow unto God?", options:["Defer not to pay it, for He hath no pleasure in fools","Wait a year before deciding","Tell it to the priests first","Write it down and seal it"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:4",verse:"When thou vowest a vow unto God, defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed.", insight:"God takes promises seriously because He keeps His; paying a vow promptly is how we resemble Him."},
{q:"What is better than vowing and not paying?", options:["Not vowing at all","Vowing much and paying late","Vowing little and often","Vowing in secret"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:5",verse:"Better is it that thou shouldest not vow, than that thou shouldest vow and not pay.", insight:"A small honest 'no promise' beats a grand broken one; God prefers plain dealing to impressive pledging."},
{q:"What should you not suffer your mouth to do?", options:["Cause thy flesh to sin","Speak against the king","Curse thy neighbor","Boast of thy riches"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:6",verse:"Suffer not thy mouth to cause thy flesh to sin; neither say thou before the angel, that it was an error: wherefore should God be angry at thy voice, and destroy the work of thine hands?", insight:"Your mouth can draft your body into sin; the tongue signs contracts the flesh must then keep."},
{q:"What should you do amid the multitude of dreams and many words?", options:["Fear thou God","Speak even more","Dream even larger","Flee to the hills"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:7",verse:"For in the multitude of dreams and many words there are also divers vanities: but fear thou God.", insight:"After all the vanities of dreams and words, one solid command remains: fear God; reverence is the anchor that holds."},
{q:"Why should you not marvel at oppression of the poor in a province?", options:["For he that is higher than the highest regardeth, and there be higher than they","For the poor deserve their lot","For the king knows nothing of it","For such things never last"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:8",verse:"If thou seest the oppression of the poor, and violent perverting of judgment and justice in a province, marvel not at the matter: for he that is higher than the highest regardeth; and there be higher than they.", insight:"Corrupt officials are watched by higher ones, and those by the Highest; no injustice happens off the record."},
{q:"Who is served by the field?", options:["The king himself","The priests alone","The merchants of the city","The armies of the land"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:9",verse:"Moreover the profit of the earth is for all: the king himself is served by the field.", insight:"Even the king eats because someone farmed; the whole pyramid of power rests on ordinary labor."},
{q:"What will never satisfy the lover of silver?", options:["Silver; nor will abundance satisfy him that loveth increase","Gold kept in secret","Bread eaten in haste","Wine drunk at feasts"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:10",verse:"He that loveth silver shall not be satisfied with silver; nor he that loveth abundance with increase: this is also vanity.", insight:"Silver never tells its lover 'enough'; money is a master that keeps raising the price of contentment."},
{q:"What happens when goods increase?", options:["They are increased that eat them","The barns overflow with plenty","The owners rest from labour","The poor are fed"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:11",verse:"When goods increase, they are increased that eat them: and what good is there to the owners thereof, saving the beholding of them with their eyes?", insight:"More goods grow more mouths; wealth multiplies its dependents faster than its satisfactions."},
{q:"What good do the owners get from increased goods?", options:["Only the beholding of them with their eyes","The love of all men","Rest for their souls","Honor in the gates"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:11",verse:"When goods increase, they are increased that eat them: and what good is there to the owners thereof, saving the beholding of them with their eyes?", insight:"The rich man's profit shrinks to looking; ownership without enjoyment is just expensive window shopping."},
{q:"Whose sleep is sweet, and whose is troubled?", options:["The labouring man's is sweet; the rich man's abundance will not suffer him to sleep","The king's is sweet; the servant's is troubled","The young man's is sweet; the old man's is troubled","The traveler's is sweet; the farmer's is troubled"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:12",verse:"The sleep of a labouring man is sweet, whether he eat little or much: but the abundance of the rich will not suffer him to sleep.", insight:"The laborer sleeps sweet on little or much while the rich man tosses; a clear conscience is the best pillow money cannot buy."},
{q:"What sore evil has the Preacher seen under the sun?", options:["Riches kept for the owners thereof to their hurt","Poverty spreading through the land","War devouring the young men","Famine emptying the barns"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:13",verse:"There is a sore evil which I have seen under the sun, namely, riches kept for the owners thereof to their hurt.", insight:"Riches can be hoarded into a wound; the same gold that could have blessed becomes the owner's injury."},
{q:"In what state does a man leave this world?", options:["Naked, as he came, carrying nothing of his labour in his hand","Clothed in fine linen, bearing his treasures","Honored by all, leaving his name behind","Wealthy still, passing riches to his son"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:15",verse:"As he came forth of his mother’s womb, naked shall he return to go as he came, and shall take nothing of his labour, which he may carry away in his hand.", insight:"We arrive with empty hands and leave the same way; everything in between is borrowed, which changes how tightly we grip."},
{q:"What profit hath he that hath laboured for the wind?", options:["None; as he came, so shall he go","Great honor among men","A portion double his brother's","Rest in his old age"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:16",verse:"And this also is a sore evil, that in all points as he came, so shall he go: and what profit hath he that hath laboured for the wind?", insight:"Laboring for the wind pays in wind; the verse asks us to check what currency our life's work is earning."},
{q:"What is good and comely for a man?", options:["To eat and drink, and enjoy the good of all his labour, for it is his portion","To gather and heap up riches for his children","To seek honor and a great name","To withdraw from the world and rest"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:18",verse:"Behold that which I have seen: it is good and comely for one to eat and to drink, and to enjoy the good of all his labour that he taketh under the sun all the days of his life, which God giveth him: for it is his portion.", insight:"Good and comely: ordinary words for an extraordinary permission to enjoy the work God gives, without apology."},
{q:"What is the gift of God to the man given riches and wealth?", options:["Power to eat thereof, to take his portion, and to rejoice in his labour","Power to rule over his neighbors","Power to increase his lands","Power to live without labour"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:19",verse:"Every man also to whom God hath given riches and wealth, and hath given him power to eat thereof, and to take his portion, and to rejoice in his labour; this is the gift of God.", insight:"Riches plus the power to enjoy them is a double gift; the second half is rarer than the first."},
{q:"Why shall he not much remember the days of his life?", options:["Because God answereth him in the joy of his heart","Because his memory fails with age","Because his days were few","Because he kept no record"], a:0, ref:"Ecclesiastes 5:20",verse:"For he shall not much remember the days of his life; because God answereth him in the joy of his heart.", insight:"Joy in the heart edits the memory of hard days; God answers a grateful life with a lightness no calendar can measure."}
]}
]);
